"Compares cumulative probabilities from two distributions on a common 0–1 scale to assess distributional agreement.\n\nUse when: A fitted cumulative distribution needs comparison with empirical cumulative probabilities. Differences near the distribution center are especially relevant.\n\nAvoid when: Detailed tail departures are the primary concern or censoring is ignored.\n\nVisual grammar: One axis encodes empirical cumulative probabilities. The other encodes model cumulative probabilities evaluated at the same ordered observations.\n\nBuild: Sort observations and select a documented empirical plotting-position rule. Evaluate the stated model CDF at those observations. Plot the paired probabilities on equal 0–1 axes with the equality diagonal.\n\nCheck: Check both coordinates are valid probabilities. Verify fitted parameters and empirical positions use the intended sample. State which axis holds model probabilities and which holds empirical probabilities.\n\nPitfalls: Probability compression makes extreme-tail differences less visible than in a Q–Q plot. Fitting the model to the same sample affects the interpretation of departures and uncertainty bands.\n\nExample: Assess a synthetic waiting-time model with empirical probabilities against fitted model CDF values and an equality reference.\n\nAlternatives: Quantile-quantile plot, ECDF, Calibration curve."
